      "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nTo be sure, since isomorphisms are always coverings\nwe see (or require) that property <MATH_0001> holds for <MATH_0002>\nif and only if it holds for any scheme <MATH_0003> isomorphic to <MATH_0004>.\nIn fact, if <MATH_0005>, or <MATH_0006>, then\nif <MATH_0007> has <MATH_0008> and <MATH_0009> is\nflat, flat and locally of finite presentation, syntomic, smooth, <ACCENT_0001>tale, or\nan open immersion, then <MATH_0010> has <MATH_0011>. This is true because\nwe can always extend <MATH_0012> to a <MATH_0013>-covering.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nWe have the following implications:\n<MATH_0014> is local in the fpqc topology\n<MATH_0015>\n<MATH_0016> is local in the fppf topology\n<MATH_0017>\n<MATH_0018> is local in the syntomic topology\n<MATH_0019>\n<MATH_0020> is local in the smooth topology\n<MATH_0021>\n<MATH_0022> is local in the <ACCENT_0002>tale topology\n<MATH_0023>\n<MATH_0024> is local in the Zariski topology.\nThis follows from\nTopologies, Lemmas\n<REF_0001>,\n<REF_0002>,\n<REF_0003>,\n<REF_0004>, and\n<REF_0005>.\n\n",
